<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Bukhara</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1785 CE · capital Bukhara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Uzbek khanate of Transoxiana under the Shaybanid and Janid dynasties, whose capital Bukhara was a leading centre of Islamic learning.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani</a>, grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan">Abu'l-Khayr</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbek-khanate">Uzbek Khanate</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurids</a> and soon conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a>. After he was killed by the Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Shah Ismail</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> in 1510, his kinsmen restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanid</a> rule in Transoxiana, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> becoming the capital in the mid-16th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a> ruled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> held by members of the dynasty and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> tribal chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-khan-ii">Abdullah Khan II</a> reunited the realm in the late 16th century, building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, bridges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>. In 1599 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janid-dynasty">Janids</a>, or Ashtarkhanids, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochid</a> line, took over. Trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> continued, and Naqshbandi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders were influential.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janid-dynasty">Janid</a> power declined, and in 1740 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghit</a> atalik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rahim-of-bukhara">Muhammad Rahim</a> took effective power, and in 1785 his relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-murad">Shah Murad</a> formally founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-bukhara">Emirate of Bukhara</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="khanate, 1500 CE – 1785 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M18 74 V58 Q50 30 82 58 V74 Z"/><path d="M42 74 V60 H58 V74"/><path d="M30 50 L50 36 L70 50"/><path d="M50 36 V26"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">khanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central Asia and the Steppe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:5.67%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1785 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bukhara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad Shaybani, Abdullah Khan II, Imam Quli Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Chagatai Turkic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bukhara Khanate, Shaybanid Khanate, Shaybanids, Janid Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=khanate-of-bukhara" aria-label="Khanate of Bukhara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6268 754 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khanate of Bukhara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6588 1010L6409 496M6588 1010L6582 1190M6588 1010L6298 1144M6588 1010L6588 1010M6588 1010L6741 986"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6409 496h0M6582 1190h0M6298 1144h0M6588 1010h0M6741 986h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6588 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6588 1010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bukhara. <a href="/atlas?year=1642&amp;focus=khanate-of-bukhara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
